A||||10||88formula||Lowered fiero handling for 88 model||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||05-16-2001||05:07 PM||||Was wondering how much better does a lowered 88 fiero handle compared to a fiero with a stock ride height? Does anyone have technical data like skip pad numbers or later g force?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||05-16-2001||05:21 PM||||I have tried to measure mine with a G-Tech but haven't found a good place to do it. Will post when done. I have springs, 17" Nitto Xtreme and all poly.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||05-16-2001||07:44 PM||||I too am very interested.  I am currently in the process of gathering parts to install new ball joints, poly all around, 1" springs, konis, 17's, and either Yokohamas or Bridgestones.  I know that my car has way more potential, because it has 155k miles on it and everything is original.  It is sloppy all over the place, so putting in new and improved stuff should be a huge step.  Let us know what you get Alex4mula.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||05-16-2001||08:12 PM||||Even if you could just give your impression of how it handles compared to the stock setup than that would be better then nothing. If you could list the suspension modifications you have that would make it easier for me to gage the results. <P>Also if you could give your impression on how your fiero rides ever bumps compared to the stock setup that would be great too.<P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||AkursedX||05-16-2001||08:40 PM||||On my '88GT I have:  <BR>KYB's all around (The white one's in the front, and the silver one's in the rear)<BR>Poly all around, including swaybar end links<BR>FieroStore Sport Springs (1 inch drop)<BR>Konig Tantrum 17x7 in the front, and 17x8 in the rear<BR>Kuhmo Ecsta Supra 712's  215/45-17 in front, 235/45-17 in the rear.<P>I found that this setup creates fairly neutral cornering, with more of a tendency to oversteer than understeer, which was a suprise to me.  Cornering is far more stable than before, but my rear struts were shot before the mods.  <BR>The ride is like a go cart, to me, it's not too bad, and I drive on northeast city streets all the time (pothole city)  and yet my cd player has never skipped once. <BR>Body roll is almost nonexsistent unless cornering very hard.  And tire break-away in cornering is very predictable.<P>Hope this helps a little.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||05-16-2001||09:32 PM||||I have almost the exact setup as AkursedX. Car rides just as he said. To me is pure fun to take every corner  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||sjp777||05-16-2001||11:21 PM||||That makes 3 of us. The only difference I have is 235/40-17's on the rear. Car sticks like glue even under extreme cornering, and body roll, what's that? My car don't do that no more.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||KissMySSFiero||05-17-2001||12:39 AM||||for anyone with the fierostore springs.  <BR>how is the ride compared to stock.  i have only heard bad things about lowering springs.  does it ride like a tank.  have they lowered much since you put them one other than the one inch advertised(how much did they settle?)  <BR>does anyone have coilovers on the rears.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>     <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>      <P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">          <BR>"When in Doubt..Whip it out!!"||||reg||1
Z||000008||sjp777||05-17-2001||01:19 AM||||Here is the car after about 9 months on Fierostore 88 springs.<P>Handles better than it ever did stock. They settled a little after the first couple months, but that was good. When I first put them on you could hardly tell it was lowered. 1" is not a big drop.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1629836&a=12458590&p=47155596&Sequence=0&res=high">       <P><BR>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||05-17-2001||06:56 PM||||The struts and shocks on my car were in good condition. They only had 47k. The suspension was stock and it actually rides better than my Tundra truck and my Grandma 89 Pontiac Bonneville ever did. I can’t remember it ever slamming over any bumps like my coupe did in the 3000 miles I drove the car. This car goes through corners like nothing I have ever driven before. Body role was non existent with my stock suspension and the car understeers barely if at all, it almost feels neutral. I went through a corner as fast as I could one day and the back end would start to break loose then I would give it more throttle and the front end would start to push. The perfect handling car in my opinion. <P>Its hard to tell when you have 17 inch low profile tires but does the car slam over bumps in the road? <P>I owned an 87 coupe once with the stock suspension and that car would slam over any bump bigger than a stick in the road and the car would push way to much in the corners and then the back end would swing out if you were not careful. I entered a corner once with my old coupe to fast and the car started to understeer. I would have been OK but I made the mistake of tapping the brakes and the whole back end of the car suddenly swung around and I did three 360 degree turns and slid into a sand doom. The force of the tires sliding through the grass jammed dirt and grass between the tire the rim.      <P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||sjp777||05-17-2001||10:12 PM||||I try to avoid bumps if at all possible, and I putt-putt over speed bumps obviously. On the few occasions I have hit large bumps, the suspension didn't bottom out. Impacts didn't seem any worse than stock suspension. Someone suggested cutting down the rubber bumpers on the front lower control arms to give you back the suspension travel you lose by lowering. I haven't done it 'cause I don't think I need it. I did cut the spacers between the bushings on the front swaybar endlinks down by about 3/4" and used shorter bolts to bring the swaybar back to the stock angle, but that's the only mod. I don't regret lowering springs at all. Now I just wonder why I didn't do it earlier.<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||14
